{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Summary of the Wikipedia page \"Raymond III Count of Tripoli\"**\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was a significant figure in the history of the Crusader states. He was born around 1140 as the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. He became Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his father's assassination. Raymond III played a crucial role in the politics of the Crusader states and the wider medieval world.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was instrumental in the politics of the Crusader states. He mediated conflicts between various Crusader lords and Muslim leaders. In 1164 he was captured by the Zengids and held captive for nearly a decade until 1173. During his captivity he lost an eye from an infection. His long imprisonment weakened his position in Tripoli but he remained a key figure upon his release.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was a central figure in the succession crisis following the assassination of King Amalric of Jerusalem in 1174. He supported the claim of Baldwin IV the Leper over the rival claim of Miles of Plancy. Raymond became regent for the young king in 1174 and played a key role in the governance of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during Baldwin IV's minority and illness. He was known for his diplomatic skills and his efforts to maintain a balance between the warring factions of the Crusader states. Raymond III also negotiated with Muslim leaders including Saladin to secure truces and maintain peace.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli died in 1187 without a male heir. His death marked the end of an era in the politics of the Crusader states. His only child was a daughter Maria who later married Balian of Ibelin. Raymond III's death left Tripoli vulnerable and contributed to the eventual fall of Jerusalem to Saladin later that year. 